      so it is a real correctness failure, not a flake) -> validated=false / excluded;
  (2) assert_correct(out_scalar, ref) PASSES on the SAME inputs -> scalar stands.

=== DIRECT SIDE-BY-SIDE DEMO (wmma vs wmma_bad vs scalar, IDENTICAL inputs; verbatim) ===
Identical inputs: A,B = bf16 uniform(-1,1) 128x128, SEED=0xC0FFEE; golden ref = G.matmul
Golden contract C: cosine>=0.999, max_rel_err<=1e-2, pcc>=0.99

path=wmma      cosine=1.000000 max_rel_err=0.000000e+00 pcc=1.000000 -> validated=True PASS golden (selected)
    driver: [polykernel-wmma] path=wmma M=128 N=128 K=128
path=wmma_bad  cosine=0.147637 max_rel_err=3.742339e+03 pcc=0.147637 -> validated=False FAIL golden (EXCLUDED, validated=false)
    driver: [polykernel-wmma] path=wmma_bad M=128 N=128 K=128
path=scalar    cosine=1.000000 max_rel_err=0.000000e+00 pcc=1.000000 -> validated=True PASS golden (selected)
    driver: [polykernel-wmma] path=scalar M=128 N=128 K=128

=== RESULT ===
On identical 128x128x128 bf16 inputs: the CORRECT WMMA variant passes golden
(cosine=pcc=1.0, bit-exact) and would be selected; the WRONG-lane-mapping variant
FAILS golden catastrophically (cosine=pcc~0.148, max_rel_err~3.7e3) and is EXCLUDED
(validated=false); the scalar baseline passes (cosine=pcc=1.0) and STANDS. This is the
correctness-gating guarantee the autotuner (Todo 25) relies on: WMMA is ADDITIVE — a
bad WMMA variant is discarded and never selected over the correct scalar baseline, so
WMMA correctness can never block the wave. Negative path CONFIRMED.
